 Fifteen passing assertions: the toolbox for solving a kata in Befunge-98.
 The pointer starts on the v above and travels down this column, where a
 space is the no-op instruction, until it meets the first > and turns east.
 Every comment line here begins with a space for exactly that reason.

 An assertion is two rows. The first pushes its whole TAP line right to
 left, so that popping it prints forwards, under a 0 that ends the print
 loop:

   v"not ok 7 - something that is true"0<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<

 The second leaves a difference that is zero when the assertion holds, adds
 the verdict to a failure count, skips the four $ that drop "not " when it
 passed, prints the line, and turns south into the next assertion:

   >"0"68*-!!:01-0g+01-0p4*j$$$$>:#,_a,$                                 v

 Nothing can be factored out, because Befunge has no functions, so those
 twenty-nine cells appear fifteen times. The last row hands the count to q,
 which is the process exit status.

 The count lives in cell (-1,0) and the scratch cells below are at (-1,1)
 and (-1,2). Negative coordinates are the only ones a program can be sure
 it will never grow over: the count was at (0,50) until this file passed
 fifty rows, at which point p was overwriting a row of the suite.
